<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mull of Kintyre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mull of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintyre">Kintyre</a> is the south-western tip of the Kintyre peninsula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. It lies only about 20 kilometres from the coast of Northern Ireland, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-channel">North Channel</a>, and has long been a crossing point between Scotland and Ireland. Ptolemy's Geography of the 2nd century names it after the Epidii, and a lighthouse has stood there since 1788.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mull-of-kintyre.jpg" alt="Mull of Kintyre" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patrick Mackie · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mull_of_Kintyre_Lighthouse_-_geograph.org.uk_-_49941.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Mull of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintyre">Kintyre</a> is the south-westernmost tip of the Kintyre peninsula, formerly spelled Cantyre, in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. It lies about 16 kilometres from Campbeltown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> and Bute and about 13 kilometres beyond Southend, the southernmost village of the peninsula, and is reached by a single-track road. The name is an anglicisation of the Gaelic Maol Chinn Tìre, meaning Rounded or Bare Headland, from maol, bald or bare, and the headland of the land. Mull is a term found chiefly in south-west Scotland for a headland or its tip, a landform bare of trees.</p><p>From the Mull the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">County Antrim</a> coast of Northern Ireland, Rathlin Island and Ailsa Craig are clearly visible, and on clearer days Malin Head in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">County Donegal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrshire">Ayrshire</a> coast can be seen. At its closest point mainland Ulster is 19 kilometres away. The Straits of Moyle, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-channel">North Channel</a>, give sea passage from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> to the Atlantic, and strong currents around the tip are a hazard to unmotorised craft.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Mull has been an important land bridge throughout history. It is thought that early humans used it on their way from continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to Ireland through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. In more recent times the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scoti">Scotti</a> crossed from Ireland to establish the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>.</p><p>A historic lighthouse, the second commissioned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, guides shipping in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-channel">North Channel</a>. The steep cliffs and hills that rise from the sea on all sides, together with frequent sea mists, have made the area a hazard to flight, and it has been the site of numerous air crashes, the remains of some of which still lie there. The best known was the crash of an RAF Chinook helicopter on 2 June 1994. On 13 June 1858 the SS New York, a steamship converted to sail, was wrecked at the south end of the Mull.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The area was made well known by the 1977 song Mull of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintyre">Kintyre</a> by Wings, the band of Paul McCartney, who lived on the peninsula. A persistent urban legend, which the British Board of Film Classification has denied, holds that an unofficial rule of the Board once measured what could be shown in film against the angle of the Kintyre peninsula on maps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mull of Kintyre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 places the Epidii in the Kintyre peninsula and neighbouring islands of Argyll, with the <b>Mull of Kintyre</b> named Epidion Akron after them.</blockquote><a href="/state/epidii">Epidii</a></li></ul></section>
